WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.320
Okay, so next up we're going to look at hooks, which is one of the kind of pro features of

00:04.320 --> 00:09.439
claw code, which most of the time you don't need. But if you suddenly think, you know what, I could

00:09.439 --> 00:14.240
really use that. It's a cool thing to know that you can do. And I would suggest that when that

00:14.240 --> 00:18.480
happens, you would then bring up claw codes docs and just refresh your memory. So I want to give

00:18.480 --> 00:22.640
you a sense of how it works and we'll make one and you'll get a sort of the gist of it,

00:22.640 --> 00:27.399
but don't feel like you have to remember all of this. It's just one of those things that if you need it, you know how to do it,

00:27.399 --> 00:29.039
you know where to go and find out more.

00:29.519 --> 00:34.659
So a hook is something which in a certain circumstance can kick off

00:34.659 --> 00:36.560
making Claude code do something.

00:36.759 --> 00:40.280
And those circumstances are called events and they're things like when Claude

00:40.280 --> 00:44.119
is about to make a tool call, maybe it's about to run a shell command,

00:44.359 --> 00:46.000
or when it's just finishing work.

00:46.000 --> 00:47.320
It just thinks I'm done now.

00:47.600 --> 00:53.000
It's moments like that, they're considered events and they can trigger a hook.

00:53.000 --> 00:57.799
They can say, "Okay, something's happened and I want to make sure that Claude Code always,

00:57.799 --> 01:02.600
when that happens, it calls something that I've fixed and advanced."

01:02.600 --> 01:06.799
That is what a hook is all about, an event triggering something that you want.

01:06.799 --> 01:12.400
So the kind of classic examples of it, there's this thing that it constantly seems

01:12.400 --> 01:18.040
to forget with me that I wanted to use a UV run instead of Python. It always goes back

01:18.040 --> 01:23.040
to Python, which is the older pre UV way of doing it. You could add a hook so that before

01:23.040 --> 01:28.659
it calls a tool, any shell script, you always check if it's doing Python something. And if

01:28.659 --> 01:34.920
so, you say no, not Python always UV. That would be an example of a time where you might want to use a hook.

01:34.920 --> 01:39.379
Another one from Claude's documentation, they have one where if it's ever stopping

01:39.379 --> 01:43.379
to ask for your permission, it could send you a notification so that you could just leave

01:43.379 --> 01:47.359
it running and go to the other room and rather than coming back two hours later and

01:47.359 --> 01:49.159
realizing it's not made any progress.

01:49.159 --> 01:50.219
It's been waiting for you.

01:50.219 --> 01:51.680
You would get some kind of notification.

01:51.680 --> 01:55.120
So that's a cool one. So things like that are time to use

01:55.120 --> 02:00.079
hooks. And then one of the absolute standard ones that are very common that we're going to use

02:00.079 --> 02:06.079
now because it's so useful is having a hook that's triggered by an event of when Claude has

02:06.079 --> 02:11.520
finished doing its work. When Claude has finished, we can seize the moment to do something else like

02:11.520 --> 02:17.000
a review. And as it happens, this is the way that the Ralph Loop thing is implemented.

02:17.000 --> 02:22.000
I believe they've done it with a hook so that as soon as it finishes work,

02:22.000 --> 02:25.000
it's then sent a new prompt that says, "Hey, I don't think you've finished.

02:25.000 --> 02:28.000
Try again." And that's how it works.

02:28.000 --> 02:29.000
So we're going to use a hook.

02:29.000 --> 02:33.000
Now we're going to set one up to do a review at the end of any piece of work.

02:33.000 --> 02:36.000
And like so many things, it could belong in a few places,

02:36.000 --> 02:38.680
but.Cloud is usually the place to go. It's

02:38.680 --> 02:44.680
in the.claud folder and you may already have a file called just settings.json. But if

